# Copy to `.env` NEXT TO compose.yaml — not into deploy/ — and fill in.
#
#   cp deploy/.env.production.example .env
#   chmod 600 .env
#
# Docker Compose reads `.env` from the directory it runs in, which is how every ${VAR} in
# compose.yaml and compose.production.yaml is resolved. The repository's .gitignore already
# ignores `.env`, and .dockerignore keeps it out of every image.
#
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THE DISTINCTION THAT MATTERS MORE THAN ANY OTHER LINE IN THIS FILE
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The variables below fall into two groups, and mixing them up is how a project publishes its
# database keys to the world.
#
#   VITE_*   BUILD-TIME, AND THEREFORE PUBLIC.
#            Vite substitutes every VITE_-prefixed value into the JavaScript bundle at build
#            time. It is not "sent to the server"; it IS the file every visitor downloads, and
#            anyone can read it with View Source.
#            => A SECRET WITH A VITE_ PREFIX IS A PUBLISHED SECRET. There is no way to un-publish
#               it except to rotate the credential.
#
#            One nuance, checked rather than assumed. It is often said that a build argument is
#            also readable in the image's history. For THIS Dockerfile it is not: the ARG and
#            ENV live only in the `builder` stage, which is discarded, and
#            `docker image history varni-dhyan:local` on the finished image lists no build
#            argument at all (verified). The values are still public — because Vite inlined
#            them into the JavaScript — but the image metadata is not the route.
#
#   the rest RUN-TIME, and private.
#            Handed to the container as environment variables when it starts, and read by the
#            node half of it. They exist
#            only in the container's memory and in this file. Nothing bakes them into an image
#            and nothing serves them to a browser.
#
# The two Supabase values that appear in BOTH groups are the same two values, and that is
# correct: the project URL and the publishable key are public by design. Row Level Security is
# what protects the data, not key secrecy — .env.example in the repository root says so at
# length. They appear twice because the browser needs them at build time and the functions need
# them at run time.
#
# The one that appears only once is the one that matters: SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY.


# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 1. BUILD-TIME — compiled into the bundle every visitor downloads. Public.
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Supabase → Project Settings → API keys.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...

# OPTIONAL, and usually best left commented out.
#
# The origin password-recovery mails send a યુવક back to. Unset, the app uses the origin the
# page is running on — production mails production, a staging host mails itself, and nothing is
# hardcoded. Set it only for the case .env.example describes: a recovery mail opened on a phone
# that never saw the host it was requested from.
#
# WHATEVER IT RESOLVES TO — set here or the live origin — must be listed under Supabase →
# Authentication → URL Configuration → Redirect URLs as `<origin>/reset-password`, or Supabase
# refuses the redirect and the link lands on the site root with no session. This is the single
# most likely thing to break on the day the domain changes. docs/RECOVERY_CONFIG.md is the
# whole dashboard checklist.
#
# Set an ORIGIN, never a full path — both apps append /reset-password themselves.
# VITE_SITE_URL=https://YOUR_DOMAIN_HERE


# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 2. RUN-TIME — the container only. Never built into an image, never sent to a browser.
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# The same project URL and publishable key as above, un-prefixed. netlify/functions/*.js read
# these from process.env; the missing VITE_ prefix is what guarantees they cannot be pulled
# into a browser bundle by accident.
SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...

# ⚠ THE ONE REAL SECRET IN THIS PROJECT.
#
# Bypasses every Row Level Security policy on every table: full read and write on all ~2,000
# યુવકોની data, and the ability to create and delete auth users. Three functions use it, each
# narrowly — login-mobile.js to resolve a mobile number to an email, create-admin.js to create
# somebody else's auth.users row, purge-test-account.js to remove a login.
#
# Rules, all of them consequences of the paragraph above:
#   * never with a VITE_ prefix, in any file, ever
#   * never as a Docker build argument
#   * never in a GitHub Actions workflow (nothing CI runs needs to read production data)
#   * never committed, and this file's real copy is `.env`, which .gitignore already covers
#   * `chmod 600 .env` on the server
#   * rotate it in the Supabase dashboard if it is ever pasted anywhere it should not be
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_...


# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 3. THE EDGE — read by compose.production.yaml and deploy/Caddyfile.
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# The bare domain: no scheme, no trailing slash, no www. The Caddyfile builds both names from
# it and redirects www to this one. Read the comment above the site block before deciding
# between apex and www — the choice binds every installed phone.
DOMAIN=YOUR_DOMAIN_HERE

# Where Let's Encrypt sends certificate-expiry warnings if automatic renewal ever stops.
ACME_EMAIL=YOUR_EMAIL_HERE


# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 4. THE IMAGE — set by deploy/deploy.sh, not by hand.
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Where the image lives. deploy/deploy.sh appends `:<tag>` to it, so this must match what the
# workflow pushes to — `ghcr.io/YOUR_GITHUB_OWNER/YOUR_REPO`, all lowercase, exactly as it
# appears under the repository's Packages tab.
#
# Required for `./deploy/deploy.sh <tag>`. Not needed for `./deploy/deploy.sh --local`.
IMAGE_REPO=ghcr.io/YOUR_GITHUB_OWNER/YOUR_REPO

# deploy.sh rewrites this line on every deploy and keeps the previous value in .deploy-history,
# which is what deploy/rollback.sh reads. LEAVE IT COMMENTED OUT — writing a value here by hand
# is how a rollback ends up restoring something that was never live. Compose falls back to
# varni-dhyan:local for a local build.
#
# APP_IMAGE=ghcr.io/YOUR_GITHUB_OWNER/YOUR_REPO:sha-abc1234

# `off` to run without the TLS edge in compose.production.yaml — for a VPS that already has its
# own reverse proxy on the host, in which case point that proxy at 127.0.0.1:8080.
# EDGE=off

# The loopback port the app container is published on. Only deploy/health-check.sh and anything
# else on the VPS itself can reach it; change it if 8080 is already taken.
# WEB_PORT=8080

# A read-only token for pulling the image if the GHCR package is private. Create at
# GitHub → Settings → Developer settings → Personal access tokens → Fine-grained, with
# `read:packages` and nothing else. Leave unset if the packages are public.
# GHCR_USER=<github-username>
# GHCR_TOKEN=github_pat_...
